A.SM=s(["\uc608\uc694","\uc774\uc608\uc694","\uc774\uc57c\uc694"],t.s)
A.b1w=new B.P("ko:g-copula",0,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc774\uc5d0\uc694/\uc608\uc694","to be (am, is, are) after a noun","Use it to say that A is B, like I'm a student or this is my bag. Add -ieyo (\uc774\uc5d0\uc694) after a noun that ends in a consonant and -yeyo after a noun that ends in a vowel (haksaeng-ieyo (\ud559\uc0dd\uc774\uc5d0\uc694), uisa-yeyo (\uc758\uc0ac\uc608\uc694)). English needs am, is, or are every time, but Korean attaches this ending to the noun itself, and the subject is often dropped when it's clear.","\uc800\ub294 \ud559\uc0dd\uc774\uc5d0\uc694.","jeoneun haksaengieyo.","I'm a student.","TOPIK 1","Don't use -yeyo (\uc608\uc694) after a consonant: haksaeng-yeyo is wrong, haksaeng-ieyo is right (\ud559\uc0dd\uc774\uc5d0\uc694).",!0,"\uc774\uc5d0\uc694",A.SM,!0,1)
A.dgd=s(["\uc740","\uc774","\uac00"],t.s)
A.b28=new B.P("ko:g-topic",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc740/\ub294","topic marker (as for ...)","Attach it to the thing you are talking about: -eun (\uc740) after a consonant and -neun (\ub294) after a vowel (jeo-neun (\uc800\ub294), oneul-eun (\uc624\ub298\uc740)). It sets the topic, like 'as for me...' or 'speaking of today...', and it often adds a feeling of contrast. English has no such particle, so learners tend to skip it or treat it as the plain subject marker.","\uc800\ub294 \ud55c\uad6d\uc5b4\ub97c \uacf5\ubd80\ud574\uc694.","jeoneun hangugeoreul gongbuhaeyo.","I study Korean.","TOPIK 1","It marks what you're talking about, not strictly who does the action, so don't think of it as just the English subject.",!0,"\ub294",A.dgd,!0,1)
A.bT2=s(["\uac00","\ub97c","\uc5d0"],t.s)
A.b2o=new B.P("ko:g-subject",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc774/\uac00","subject marker (who or what does it)","Attach it to the person or thing that does the action or has the quality: -i (\uc774) after a consonant and -ga (\uac00) after a vowel (dongsaeng-i (\ub3d9\uc0dd\uc774), bi-ga (\ube44\uac00)). It often introduces new information, like 'it's raining' or 'a cat is sleeping'. English shows the subject by word order, but Korean needs a particle, and it drops the subject word completely when it's obvious. Note that jeo becomes je-ga (\uc81c\uac00) for \"I\" (je-ga, not jeo-ga).","\ub3d9\uc0dd\uc774 \ud559\uad50\uc5d0 \uac00\uc694.","dongsaengi hakgyoe gayo.","My younger sibling goes to school.","TOPIK 1","Use -i/-ga (\uc774/\uac00) for new information and -eun/-neun (\uc740/\ub294) for the topic, so the same noun can take either one.",!0,"\uc774",A.bT2,!0,1)
A.Ta=s(["\uc744","\uc5d0","\uc774"],t.s)
A.b1l=new B.P("ko:g-object",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc744/\ub97c","object marker (the thing acted on)","Attach it to the thing that receives the action: -eul (\uc744) after a consonant and -reul (\ub97c) after a vowel (chaek-eul (\ucc45\uc744), keopi-reul (\ucee4\ud53c\ub97c)). The order is object first and verb last, so 'I drink coffee' becomes 'coffee-reul drink'. In casual speech people often drop it, but learners should keep it so the sentence stays clear.","\uc800\ub294 \ucee4\ud53c\ub97c \ub9c8\uc154\uc694.","jeoneun keopireul masyeoyo.","I drink coffee.","TOPIK 1","Don't copy English word order: the verb always comes last, after the object.",!0,"\ub97c",A.Ta,!0,1)
A.e_N=s(["\ub97c","\uac00","\uc774"],t.s)
A.b1e=new B.P("ko:g-at-ni",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc5d0","at / in / to (time, place, destination)","Use it after a time (7 o'clock, weekends), after the place where something is (with isseoyo (\uc788\uc5b4\uc694)), and after the place you go to (hakgyo-e gayo (\ud559\uad50\uc5d0 \uac00\uc694)). It never changes shape, whatever the last sound of the noun is. English uses different words (at, on, in, to), but Korean uses -e (\uc5d0) for all of these, though words like yesterday, today, tomorrow, and now take no particle (eoje (\uc5b4\uc81c), not eoje-e).","\uc800\ub294 \uc77c\uacf1 \uc2dc\uc5d0 \uc77c\uc5b4\ub098\uc694.","jeoneun ilgop sie ireonayo.","I get up at seven.","TOPIK 1","Don't use -e (\uc5d0) for where an action happens, since that needs -eseo (\uc5d0\uc11c).",!0,"\uc5d0",A.e_N,!0,1)
A.UZ=s(["\uc5d0","\ub97c","\ub85c"],t.s)
A.aYk=new B.P("ko:g-at-eseo",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc5d0\uc11c","at / in (where an action happens)","Use it after a place where you do an action, like studying, eating, or working (doseogwan-eseo gongbuhaeyo (\ub3c4\uc11c\uad00\uc5d0\uc11c \uacf5\ubd80\ud574\uc694)). It also means 'from' in some sentences, but at first learn the 'at' meaning. English uses at or in for both places and actions, but Korean splits them: -e (\uc5d0) is for where something is or goes, and -eseo (\uc5d0\uc11c) is for where something is done.","\uc800\ub294 \ub3c4\uc11c\uad00\uc5d0\uc11c \uacf5\ubd80\ud574\uc694.","jeoneun doseogwaneseo gongbuhaeyo.","I study at the library.","TOPIK 1","Compare isseoyo (\uc788\uc5b4\uc694) with -e (it's in the room) and action verbs with -eseo (I study in the room).",!0,"\uc5d0\uc11c",A.UZ,!0,1)
A.dDZ=s(["\uba39\uc544\uc694","\uba39\uc694","\uba39\uc73c\uc694"],t.s)
A.aZ5=new B.P("ko:g-polite-present",2,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc544\uc694/\uc5b4\uc694","polite present tense","Use it for everyday polite speech about now, habits, and often the near future. Take the verb or adjective stem and add -ayo (\uc544\uc694) if its last vowel is a or o (joh-ayo (\uc88b\uc544\uc694)), and -eoyo (\uc5b4\uc694) for all other vowels (meog-eoyo (\uba39\uc5b4\uc694)); hada verbs become haeyo. The same form works for statements and questions, so only your tone changes, and it doesn't change for I, you, or they like English verbs do.","\uc800\ub294 \ub9e4\uc77c \ubc25\uc744 \uba39\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun maeil babeul meogeoyo.","I eat rice every day.","TOPIK 1","The vowel rule only looks at the last vowel of the stem, so it's -ayo (\uc544\uc694) for a and o and -eoyo (\uc5b4\uc694) for everything else.",!0,"\uba39\uc5b4\uc694",A.dDZ,!0,1)
A.A1=s(["\uc54a","\uc548\uc774","\uc5c6"],t.s)
A.aZL=new B.P("ko:g-an-neg",3,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc548 + \ub3d9\uc0ac/\ud615\uc6a9\uc0ac","short negation (don't / isn't)","Put an (\uc548) right before a verb or adjective to say you don't do something or something isn't so (an meogeoyo (\uc548 \uba39\uc5b4\uc694)). It's the short, spoken negative, and it works for normal verbs and adjectives alike. With hada verbs, put it between the noun and haeyo: gongbu-reul an haeyo (\uacf5\ubd80\ub97c \uc548 \ud574\uc694). Don't mix it up with mot (\ubabb), which means can't.","\uc800\ub294 \ucee4\ud53c\ub97c \uc548 \ub9c8\uc154\uc694.","jeoneun keopireul an masyeoyo.","I don't drink coffee.","TOPIK 1","An (\uc548) goes in front of the verb, not at the end of the sentence like English not.",!0,"\uc548",A.A1,!0,1)
A.ccy=s(["\ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc548\uc544\uc694","\ub9c8\uc2dc \uc54a\uc544\uc694","\ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc5b4\uc694"],t.s)
A.b4c=new B.P("ko:g-jianayo",3,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694","long negation (don't / isn't)","Attach -ji anayo (\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694) to the verb or adjective stem (mashi-ji anayo (\ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694), chup-ji anayo (\ucda5\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694)). It means the same as the short an (\uc548) negative but sounds a little more formal, and people use it a lot in writing. It's spelled with an h (anh-), but the h is silent, so it sounds like anayo.","\uc800\ub294 \uc6b0\uc720\ub97c \ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694.","jeoneun uyureul masiji anayo.","I don't drink milk.","TOPIK 1","Use the bare stem before -ji (\uc9c0), not the polite form (mashi-ji anayo (\ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694), not masyeoyo-ji anayo).",!0,"\ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694",A.ccy,!0,1)
A.dCd=s(["\ub9cc\ub0ac\uc544\uc694","\ub9cc\ub098\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694","\ub9cc\ub098\uc558\uc5b4\uc694"],t.s)
A.aZR=new B.P("ko:g-past",4,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc558\uc5b4\uc694/\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694","polite past tense","Use it to say something already happened. Take the stem and add -ass-eoyo (\uc558\uc5b4\uc694) if the last vowel is a or o, and -eoss-eoyo (\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694) for other vowels; hada verbs become haess-eoyo (\ud588\uc5b4\uc694). English changes the verb to the past (ate, went), but Korean adds this ending to the stem, and it stays the same for I, you, and they. After a vowel, the stem and ass merge into one syllable: manna + ass-eoyo = mannass-eoyo (\ub9cc\ub0ac\uc5b4\uc694).","\uc5b4\uc81c \uce5c\uad6c\ub97c \ub9cc\ub0ac\uc5b4\uc694.","eoje chingureul mannasseoyo.","I met a friend yesterday.","TOPIK 1","The ss comes before the polite ending, so don't just add -eoyo (\uc5b4\uc694) to the present form.",!0,"\ub9cc\ub0ac\uc5b4\uc694",A.dCd,!0,1)
A.e4R=s(["\ub9cc\ub098\uc744 \uac70\uc608\uc694","\ub9cc\ub0a0 \uac70\uc5d0\uc694","\ub9cc\ub098 \uac70\uc608\uc694"],t.s)
A.b1Z=new B.P("ko:g-future",4,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\u3139 \uac70\uc608\uc694","will / going to (plans and guesses)","Use it for plans and for guesses, like I'm going to... or it will... After a vowel stem add -l geoyeyo (man-nal geoyeyo (\ub9cc\ub0a0 \uac70\uc608\uc694)), and after a consonant stem add -eul geoyeyo (\uc744 \uac70\uc608\uc694) (ilg-eul geoyeyo (\uc77d\uc744 \uac70\uc608\uc694)). Stems that already end in l just add geoyeyo (sal geoyeyo (\uc0b4 \uac70\uc608\uc694)). English uses will or going to, but Korean keeps the verb and only changes the ending.","\ub0b4\uc77c \uce5c\uad6c\ub97c \ub9cc\ub0a0 \uac70\uc608\uc694.","naeil chingureul mannal geoyeyo.","I'm going to meet a friend tomorrow.","TOPIK 1","The standard spelling is geoyeyo (\uac70\uc608\uc694), and the common spelling geoeyo (\uac70\uc5d0\uc694) is nonstandard, so don't use it.",!0,"\ub9cc\ub0a0 \uac70\uc608\uc694",A.e4R,!0,1)
A.Ph=s(["\uc788\uc544\uc694","\uc788\uc694","\uc788\uc73c\uc694"],t.s)
A.aZz=new B.P("ko:g-iss-eops",5,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc788\uc5b4\uc694/\uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694","there is / there isn't; to have / not have","Use isseoyo (\uc788\uc5b4\uc694) for 'there is' and 'I have', and eopseoyo (\uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694) for 'there isn't' and 'I don't have'. Say the place or the owner first, then the thing with -i/-ga (\uc774/\uac00): jib-e goyangi-ga isseoyo (\uc9d1\uc5d0 \uace0\uc591\uc774\uac00 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694). Korean usually has no separate word for have, so these two words do the job. Don't make the negative with an, because eopseoyo is its own opposite.","\uc9d1\uc5d0 \uace0\uc591\uc774\uac00 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694.","jibe goyangiga isseoyo.","There's a cat at home.","TOPIK 1","The negative of isseoyo (\uc788\uc5b4\uc694) is eopseoyo (\uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694), not an isseoyo.",!0,"\uc788\uc5b4\uc694",A.Ph,!0,1)
A.deW=s(["\uc774\uac83","\uc774\uac8c","\uc774\uac70"],t.s)
A.b3Z=new B.P("ko:g-demonstrative",6,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc774/\uadf8/\uc800 + \uba85\uc0ac","this / that / that over there + noun","Put i (\uc774), geu (\uadf8), or jeo (\uc800) right before a noun: i chaek (\uc774 \ucc45, this book), geu chaek (that book), jeo chaek (that book over there). I means near the speaker, geu means near the listener or something you both just mentioned, and jeo means far from both. English has two (this, that) but Korean has three, and there is no word for a or the.","\uc774 \ucc45\uc740 \uc7ac\ubbf8\uc788\uc5b4\uc694.","i chaegeun jaemiisseoyo.","This book is fun.","TOPIK 1","Don't add a particle between the word and the noun, so it's i chaek (\uc774 \ucc45), not i-neun chaek.",!0,"\uc774",A.deW,!0,1)
A.euy=s(["\uba39\uc5b4\uace0","\uba39\uc73c\uace0","\uba39\ub294\uace0"],t.s)
A.aZB=new B.P("ko:g-and-go",7,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uace0","and / and then (joining two actions)",'Attach -go (\uace0) straight to the verb stem to join two actions in order, like "and" or "and then" in English. It works the same with or without a final consonant, so you never change the stem. In "and then" sequences, the tense and the polite ending usually go only on the last verb.',"\uc800\ub294 \ubc25\uc744 \uba39\uace0 \ucee4\ud53c\ub97c \ub9c8\uc154\uc694.","jeoneun babeul meokgo keopireul masyeoyo.","I eat a meal and then drink coffee.","TOPIK 1","Attach -go (\uace0) directly to the stem, and usually put the tense only on the last verb.",!0,"\uba39\uace0",A.euy,!0,1)
A.eHf=s(["\uce5c\uad6c\ub97c\ud558\uace0","\uce5c\uad6c\uc640\uace0","\uce5c\uad6c\uc5d0\ud558\uace0"],t.s)
A.b_P=new B.P("ko:g-with-hago",7,"\ucd08\uae09","\ud558\uace0 / \uc640 / \uacfc","and, with (joining nouns)",'Put hago (\ud558\uace0), wa (\uc640) or gwa (\uacfc) between two nouns to say "A and B," or after a person to say "with." Hago works after any noun and is the easiest and most common in speech. Wa follows a word that ends in a vowel and gwa follows a word that ends in a consonant, and they sound a bit more formal or written.',"\uc800\ub294 \uce5c\uad6c\ud558\uace0 \uc601\ud654\ub97c \ubd10\uc694.","jeoneun chinguhago yeonghwareul bwayo.","I watch a movie with a friend.","TOPIK 1","Use wa (\uc640) after a vowel and gwa (\uacfc) after a consonant, but hago (\ud558\uace0) works after anything.",!0,"\uce5c\uad6c\ud558\uace0",A.eHf,!0,1)
A.emQ=s(["\ucee4\ud53c\ub3c4\ub97c","\ucee4\ud53c\ub97c\ub3c4","\ucee4\ud53c\uac00\ub3c4"],t.s)
A.b17=new B.P("ko:g-also-do",7,"\ucd08\uae09","\ub3c4","also, too",'Add -do (\ub3c4) to a noun to say "also" or "too" about that noun. It takes the place of the particles i/ga (\uc774/\uac00), eun/neun (\uc740/\ub294) and eul/reul (\uc744/\ub97c), so don\'t stack them (not "coffee-do-reul"). It has the same form after a vowel or a consonant, and it attaches right after the noun it points to.',"\uc800\ub294 \ucee4\ud53c\ub3c4 \uc88b\uc544\ud574\uc694.","jeoneun keopido joahaeyo.","I like coffee too.","TOPIK 1","The particle -do (\ub3c4) replaces the subject and object particles instead of sitting next to them.",!0,"\ucee4\ud53c\ub3c4",A.emQ,!0,1)
A.cfM=s(["\ud558\ub098 \uac1c","\uc14b \uac1c","\uc0bc \uac1c"],t.s)
A.b14=new B.P("ko:g-counters",8,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc218 \uc138\uae30: \uac1c\xb7\uba85\xb7\uc794","counting things and people with counters",'Korean puts a number and a counter after the noun: apples "se gae (\uc138 \uac1c)" (3 things), friends "du myeong (\ub450 \uba85)" (2 people), coffee "han jan (\ud55c \uc794)" (1 cup). Use native numbers (hana (\ud558\ub098), dul (\ub458), set (\uc14b), net (\ub137)) in a short form before counters: han, du, se, ne, and then daseot, yeoseot and so on. Use Sino numbers (il (\uc77c), i (\uc774), sam (\uc0bc)) for dates, money, minutes and phone numbers. English doesn\'t need this extra counter word, so it\'s easy to forget.',"\uc0ac\uacfc \uc138 \uac1c\ub97c \uc0c0\uc5b4\uc694.","sagwa se gaereul sasseoyo.","I bought three apples.","TOPIK 1","Native numbers go with counters like gae (\uac1c), myeong (\uba85) and jan (\uc794), and Sino numbers go with money and dates.",!0,"\uc138 \uac1c",A.cfM,!0,1)
A.dvQ=s(["\uc77d\uc138\uc694","\uc77d\uc5b4\uc138\uc694","\uc77d\uc2dc\uc694"],t.s)
A.aZm=new B.P("ko:g-honorific-seyo",9,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\uc138\uc694","polite honorific present, also a polite request",'Add -seyo (\uc138\uc694) to a verb stem that ends in a vowel, and -euseyo (\uc73c\uc138\uc694) to a stem that ends in a consonant, to show respect to the person doing the action. Use it for teachers, parents, elders or strangers, and never for yourself. It also works as a soft request or command, like "please" in English. Korean often leaves out the subject, so the verb ending shows who you\'re respecting.',"\uc120\uc0dd\ub2d8\uc740 \uc9c0\uae08 \ucc45\uc744 \uc77d\uc73c\uc138\uc694.","seonsaengnimeun jigeum chaegeul ilgeuseyo.","The teacher is reading a book now.","TOPIK 1","Never use -seyo (\uc138\uc694) for your own actions, only for the person you respect.",!0,"\uc77d\uc73c\uc138\uc694",A.dvQ,!0,1)
A.cNS=s(["\ub9d0\ud558 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694","\ub9d0\ud574 \uc8fc\uc2dc\uc694","\ub9d0\ud574\uc694 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694"],t.s)
A.b1_=new B.P("ko:g-juseyo",9,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc544/\uc5b4 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694","please do (something) for me",'Add -a/eo juseyo (\uc544/\uc5b4 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694) to a verb to ask someone to do something for you, like "please ..." or "could you ..." in English. Take the polite -a/eo form of the verb (open becomes yeoreo (\uc5f4\uc5b4), write becomes sseo (\uc368)) and add juseyo after a space. With a plain noun you can just say the noun plus juseyo, as in "mul juseyo (\ubb3c \uc8fc\uc138\uc694)" for "water, please." It\'s polite enough for shops, restaurants and strangers.',"\ucc9c\ucc9c\ud788 \ub9d0\ud574 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694.","cheoncheonhi malhae juseyo.","Please speak slowly.","TOPIK 1","Use the -a/eo (\uc544/\uc5b4) form before juseyo (\uc8fc\uc138\uc694), not the bare stem.",!0,"\ub9d0\ud574 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694",A.cNS,!0,1)
A.bO9=s(["\uac00\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\ud558\uc694","\uac00\uc11c \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694","\uac00\uace0 \uc88b\uc544\uc694"],t.s)
A.aZx=new B.P("ko:g-want",9,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694","want to (do something)",'Attach -go sipeoyo (\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694) to a verb stem to say you want to do something, like "I want to ..." in English. The stem doesn\'t change, whether it ends in a vowel or a consonant. It\'s for your own wishes, so with "he/she wants to" use -go sipeohaeyo (\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\ud574\uc694). English says "want to go" with a separate verb, but Korean usually attaches an ending to the verb instead.',"\uc800\ub294 \ud55c\uad6d\uc5d0 \uac00\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun hanguge gago sipeoyo.","I want to go to Korea.","TOPIK 1","For another person's wish, use -go sipeohaeyo (\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\ud574\uc694) instead of -go sipeoyo (\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694).",!0,"\uac00\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694",A.bO9,!0,1)
A.ewr=s(["\uc77d\ub294 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694","\uc77d\uc744 \uc218 \uc788\uc694","\uc77d\uc744 \uc218 \uc774\uc5d0\uc694"],t.s)
A.b0C=new B.P("ko:g-can",10,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\u3139 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694/\uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694","can / cannot (ability or possibility)",'Add -l su isseoyo to a verb stem that ends in a vowel, or -eul su isseoyo (\uc744 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694) to one that ends in a consonant, to say you can do something. Use eopseoyo (\uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694) instead of isseoyo to say you can\'t. It covers both "I know how to" and "it\'s possible to," which English splits into can and be able to. Stems that end in the letter l (like "nolda (\ub180\ub2e4)," to play) keep it and just take su, as in nol su isseoyo (\ub180 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694).',"\uc800\ub294 \ud55c\uad6d\uc5b4\ub97c \uc77d\uc744 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun hangugeoreul ilgeul su isseoyo.","I can read Korean.","TOPIK 1","Use su isseoyo (\uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694) for can and su eopseoyo (\uc218 \uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694) for cannot, and keep su separate with spaces.",!0,"\uc77d\uc744 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694",A.ewr,!0,1)
A.csg=s(["\uba39 \ubabb \ud574\uc694","\uba39\uc5b4 \ubabb\uc694","\ubabb \uba39\ub294\uc694"],t.s)
A.aZ3=new B.P("ko:g-mot",10,"\ucd08\uae09","\ubabb + \ub3d9\uc0ac","can't / unable to (before a verb)",'Put mot (\ubabb) right before a verb to say you can\'t do it, because of ability or circumstances. It differs from an (just "don\'t"): mot means you want to or should but can\'t. With verbs like "hada" (to do), it goes between the noun and the verb, as in "suyeong mot haeyo (\uc218\uc601 \ubabb \ud574\uc694)." It\'s a short, common way to say "can\'t" in everyday talk.',"\uc800\ub294 \ub9e4\uc6b4 \uc74c\uc2dd\uc744 \ubabb \uba39\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun maeun eumsigeul mot meogeoyo.","I can't eat spicy food.","TOPIK 1","Mot (\ubabb) goes right before the verb, so for 'noun + hada' put it between the noun and hada.",!0,"\ubabb \uba39\uc5b4\uc694",A.csg,!0,1)
A.caU=s(["\uce5c\uad6c\uc5d0","\uce5c\uad6c\ub97c","\uce5c\uad6c\uc5d0\uc11c"],t.s)
A.b_a=new B.P("ko:g-to-person",11,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc5d0\uac8c / \ud55c\ud14c","to (a person or animal)",'Put hante (\ud55c\ud14c, casual and common in speech) or ege (\uc5d0\uac8c, a bit more written) after a person to mean "to" them, like when giving, calling or writing. Use e (\uc5d0) for places and things, not people ("to school," not "to a friend"). For someone you want to show respect to, such as a teacher or grandparent, use kke (\uaed8). English often drops "to" (give me a gift), but Korean always needs this particle.',"\uc800\ub294 \uce5c\uad6c\ud55c\ud14c \uc120\ubb3c\uc744 \uc92c\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun chinguhante seonmureul jwosseoyo.","I gave a gift to my friend.","TOPIK 1","Use hante (\ud55c\ud14c) or ege (\uc5d0\uac8c) for people and animals, and e (\uc5d0) only for places and things.",!0,"\uce5c\uad6c\ud55c\ud14c",A.caU,!0,1)
A.eQW=s(["\ub9cc\ub098\ub85c \uac00\uc694","\ub9cc\ub098\ub7ec\uc11c \uac00\uc694","\ub9cc\ub098\uace0\ub7ec \uac00\uc694"],t.s)
A.aYy=new B.P("ko:g-go-to-do",11,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\ub7ec \uac00\uc694","go in order to (do)",'Add -reo (\ub7ec) to a verb stem that ends in a vowel, or -eureo (\uc73c\ub7ec) to one that ends in a consonant, and then gayo (\uac00\uc694) (or oayo (\uc640\uc694)) to say you go somewhere to do something. Stems that end in the letter l take -reo, like "nolda" becoming "nolreo gayo (\ub180\ub7ec \uac00\uc694)." A movement verb such as go or come has to follow it. English says "go to see" or "go and see," while Korean uses this one pattern.',"\uc800\ub294 \uce5c\uad6c\ub97c \ub9cc\ub098\ub7ec \uac00\uc694.","jeoneun chingureul mannareo gayo.","I'm going to meet a friend.","TOPIK 1","Only a go or come verb can follow -(eu)reo.",!0,"\ub9cc\ub098\ub7ec \uac00\uc694",A.eQW,!0,1)
A.ePB=s(["\uba39\uc744\uac00\uc694","\uba39\ub294\uae4c\uc694","\uba39\uc744\uae4c\uc544\uc694"],t.s)
A.b1B=new B.P("ko:g-shall-we",12,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\u3139\uae4c\uc694?","shall we...? / should I...?",'Add -lkkayo to a verb stem that ends in a vowel, or -eulkkayo (\uc744\uae4c\uc694) to one that ends in a consonant, to suggest something or offer to do something, like "Shall we ...?" or "Should I ...?" in English. Stems that end in the letter l just add -kkayo, such as "yeolda (\uc5f4\ub2e4)" becoming "yeolkkayo (\uc5f4\uae4c\uc694)." The usual answer is "joayo (\uc88b\uc544\uc694)" (sounds good). It can also mean "I wonder if ..." about something you\'re unsure of.',"\uac19\uc774 \uc810\uc2ec \uba39\uc744\uae4c\uc694?","gachi jeomsim meogeulkkayo?","Shall we have lunch together?","TOPIK 1","It's a suggestion or an offer, so the usual answer is joayo (\uc88b\uc544\uc694) or ne (\ub124).",!0,"\uba39\uc744\uae4c\uc694",A.ePB,!0,1)
A.c2Q=s(["\uc624\uc544\uc11c","\uc624\uc5b4\uc11c","\uc654\uc5b4\uc11c"],t.s)
A.b1d=new B.P("ko:g-because-aseo",12,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc544\uc11c/\uc5b4\uc11c","because, so (reason)",'Add -aseo (\uc544\uc11c) after a verb or adjective stem whose last vowel is a or o, and -eoseo (\uc5b4\uc11c) after any other stem. Verbs that end in hada become haeseo (\ud574\uc11c), and stems ending in eu (like gopeuda) drop it and follow the vowel before it (gopaseo (\uace0\ud30c\uc11c)). It gives the reason, like "because" or "so" in English, and the reason comes first. Don\'t put the past tense inside it (not -asseoseo or -eosseoseo), and don\'t follow it with a command or "let\'s" (the result part can\'t be a request).',"\ube44\uac00 \uc640\uc11c \uc9d1\uc5d0 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694.","biga waseo jibe isseoyo.","It's raining, so I stay home.","TOPIK 1","Keep the reason in the present form, and don't follow it with a command or a suggestion.",!0,"\uc640\uc11c",A.c2Q,!0,1)
A.Lo=s(["\ub85c","\ub97c","\uac00"],t.s)
A.b0_=new B.P("ko:g-from-until",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc5d0\uc11c/\ubd80\ud130 ~ \uae4c\uc9c0","from ... to ... (place or time)","Use -eseo (\uc5d0\uc11c) for the starting place and -buteo (\ubd80\ud130) for the starting time, then add -kkaji (\uae4c\uc9c0) for the end point (jip-eseo hakgyo-kkaji (\uc9d1\uc5d0\uc11c \ud559\uad50\uae4c\uc9c0), ahop si-buteo (\uc544\ud649 \uc2dc\ubd80\ud130)). You can use -buteo (\ubd80\ud130) and -kkaji (\uae4c\uc9c0) alone too, like 'until Friday' or 'from now'. English uses from and to for both, but Korean picks the particle by place or time, and there's no space before the particle.","\uc800\ub294 \uc544\ud649 \uc2dc\ubd80\ud130 \uc5ec\uc12f \uc2dc\uae4c\uc9c0 \uc77c\ud574\uc694.","jeoneun ahop sibuteo yeoseot sikkaji ilhaeyo.","I work from nine to six.","TOPIK 1","For places use -eseo (\uc5d0\uc11c) and for times use -buteo (\ubd80\ud130), and both pair with -kkaji (\uae4c\uc9c0) for 'until' or 'to'.",!0,"\ubd80\ud130",A.Lo,!0,1)
A.UB=s(["\uc73c\ub85c","\ub97c","\uac00"],t.s)
A.b3a=new B.P("ko:g-direction-ro",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc73c\ub85c/\ub85c","to / toward (direction), by / with (means)","Use it for the direction you move in (oreunjjok-euro (\uc624\ub978\ucabd\uc73c\ub85c)) and for the tool or means you use (beoseu-ro (\ubc84\uc2a4\ub85c), jihacheol-ro (\uc9c0\ud558\ucca0\ub85c)). Add -euro (\uc73c\ub85c) after a consonant and -ro (\ub85c) after a vowel or after \u3139 (\u3139). English says by bus or toward the right with different words, but Korean uses the same particle for both.","\uc800\ub294 \ubc84\uc2a4\ub85c \ud559\uad50\uc5d0 \uac00\uc694.","jeoneun beoseuro hakgyoe gayo.","I go to school by bus.","TOPIK 1","Don't use -euro (\uc73c\ub85c) after a vowel: beoseu-euro is wrong, beoseu-ro is right (\ubc84\uc2a4\ub85c).",!0,"\ub85c",A.UB,!0,1)
A.eY6=s(["\ub97c","\uc5d0\uc11c","\uc5d0"],t.s)
A.b22=new B.P("ko:g-possessive-ui",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc758","possessive marker (of, ...'s)","Put it between the owner and the thing owned: chinguui chaek (\uce5c\uad6c\uc758 \ucc45) is 'a friend's book'. Attach -ui (\uc758) right after the owner with no change for consonants or vowels. In speech it's often shortened or dropped, and je (\uc81c) is used instead of jeoui (\uc800\uc758) for 'my'. English puts 's after the owner, but Korean puts a particle in the same place.","\uc800\ub294 \uce5c\uad6c\uc758 \ucc45\uc744 \uc77d\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun chinguui chaegeul ilgeoyo.","I'm reading my friend's book.","TOPIK 1","Don't forget that jeo (\uc800) and na (\ub098) shorten, so 'my bag' is usually je gabang (\uc81c \uac00\ubc29), and -ui (\uc758) is often left out.",!0,"\uc758",A.eY6,!0,1)
A.eFW=s(["\uc5d0\uc11c","\ud55c\ud14c\ub97c","\uc5d0\uac8c\ub97c"],t.s)
A.b47=new B.P("ko:g-from-person",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc5d0\uac8c\uc11c/\ud55c\ud14c\uc11c","from (a person)","Use it after a person when something comes from them, like a gift, a call, or a message (chingu-hanteseo (\uce5c\uad6c\ud55c\ud14c\uc11c), eomeoni-egeseo (\uc5b4\uba38\ub2c8\uc5d0\uac8c\uc11c)). -hanteseo (\ud55c\ud14c\uc11c) is more casual and -egeseo (\uc5d0\uac8c\uc11c) a little more formal. English says from for both places and people, but Korean uses -eseo (\uc5d0\uc11c) only for places, so a person needs -egeseo (\uc5d0\uac8c\uc11c) or -hanteseo (\ud55c\ud14c\uc11c).","\uc800\ub294 \uce5c\uad6c\ud55c\ud14c\uc11c \uc120\ubb3c\uc744 \ubc1b\uc558\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun chinguhanteseo seonmureul badasseoyo.","I got a gift from a friend.","TOPIK 1","Don't use plain -eseo (\uc5d0\uc11c) after a person, since that's only for places.",!0,"\ud55c\ud14c\uc11c",A.eFW,!0,1)
A.cnr=s(["\ubc16\uc5d0","\ubfd0","\uc774\ub9cc"],t.s)
A.b0N=new B.P("ko:g-only-man",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\ub9cc","only, just","Attach it right after the noun you want to single out: keopi-man (\ucee4\ud53c\ub9cc) means 'only coffee'. It takes the same shape after any sound, and it replaces -i/-ga (\uc774/\uac00) and -eul/-reul (\uc744/\ub97c), but goes after -e (\uc5d0), -eseo (\uc5d0\uc11c) and similar (jib-e-man (\uc9d1\uc5d0\ub9cc)). Unlike English only, which moves around the sentence, Korean attaches -man (\ub9cc) to the exact word it limits.","\uc800\ub294 \ucee4\ud53c\ub9cc \ub9c8\uc154\uc694.","jeoneun keopiman masyeoyo.","I only drink coffee.","TOPIK 1","Don't use -bakke (\ubc16\uc5d0) here, since it needs a negative ending and -man (\ub9cc) doesn't.",!0,"\ub9cc",A.cnr,!0,1)
A.ffx=s(["\uc5d0\uc11c","\ub97c","\uc5d0\uac8c"],t.s)
A.b06=new B.P("ko:g-than-boda",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\ubcf4\ub2e4","than (comparison)","Put it after the thing you compare against: eoje-boda deowoyo (\uc5b4\uc81c\ubcf4\ub2e4 \ub354\uc6cc\uc694) is 'hotter than yesterday'. It has the same shape after any sound, and deo (\ub354, 'more') in front of the adjective is common but not required. English says 'than' after the adjective, but Korean puts -boda (\ubcf4\ub2e4) before the adjective, and the thing being compared first comes with -eun/-neun (\uc740/\ub294) or -i/-ga (\uc774/\uac00).","\uc624\ub298\uc740 \uc5b4\uc81c\ubcf4\ub2e4 \ub354\uc6cc\uc694.","oneureun eojeboda deowoyo.","Today is hotter than yesterday.","TOPIK 1","The comparison word comes before the adjective in Korean, so don't copy the English order of 'hotter than yesterday'.",!0,"\ubcf4\ub2e4",A.ffx,!0,1)
A.fad=s(["\uba39\uc5b4 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694","\uba39\ub294 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694","\uba39\uace0 \uc788\uc694"],t.s)
A.b1V=new B.P("ko:g-progressive",4,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uace0 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694","am/is/are doing (in progress)","Use it for an action that's happening right now or in progress. Take the verb stem and add -go isseoyo (\uace0 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694) (meog-go isseoyo (\uba39\uace0 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694)), with no vowel harmony or consonant rule. English uses be + -ing, and Korean has the same idea, but some verbs like ipda (\uc785\ub2e4, 'to wear') and salda (\uc0b4\ub2e4, 'to live') use it for a lasting state.","\uc9c0\uae08 \ubc25\uc744 \uba39\uace0 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694.","jigeum babeul meokgo isseoyo.","I'm eating right now.","TOPIK 1","Don't change the stem: it's always the bare stem plus -go (\uace0), not -eo (\uc5b4) or -neun (\ub294).",!0,"\uba39\uace0 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694",A.fad,!0,1)
A.MW=s(["\uc600\uc5b4\uc694","\uc774\uc600\uc5b4\uc694","\uc774\uc5c7\uc5b4\uc694"],t.s)
A.b3c=new B.P("ko:g-past-copula",4,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc774\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694/\uc600\uc5b4\uc694","was / were (after a noun)","Use it to say that A was B. Add -ieosseoyo (\uc774\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694) after a noun that ends in a consonant (hyuil-ieosseoyo (\ud734\uc77c\uc774\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694)) and the usual form after a vowel is -yeosseoyo (\uc600\uc5b4\uc694) (gasu-yeosseoyo (\uac00\uc218\uc600\uc5b4\uc694)). It's the past of -ieyo/-yeyo (\uc774\uc5d0\uc694/\uc608\uc694). English changes am/is to was, and Korean changes the ending, with the subject dropped when it's clear.","\uc5b4\uc81c\ub294 \ud734\uc77c\uc774\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694.","eojeneun hyuirieosseoyo.","Yesterday was a day off.","TOPIK 1","Don't write -iyeosseoyo (\uc774\uc600\uc5b4\uc694), since the correct spellings are -ieosseoyo (\uc774\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694) and -yeosseoyo (\uc600\uc5b4\uc694).",!0,"\uc774\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694",A.MW,!0,1)
A.VB=s(["\ubcf8 \uc801\uc744","\ubcf8 \uc801\uac00","\ubcf8 \uc801\uc5d0"],t.s)
A.b3W=new B.P("ko:g-experience",4,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc544/\uc5b4 \ubcf8 \uc801\uc774 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694","have done ... before (experience)","Use it to say you have tried something at least once. Take the verb stem, add -a/-eo bon (\uc544/\uc5b4 \ubcf8) with the same vowel rule as -ayo/-eoyo (\uc544\uc694/\uc5b4\uc694), then add jeok-i isseoyo (\uc801\uc774 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694) (ga bon jeok-i isseoyo (\uac00 \ubcf8 \uc801\uc774 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694)). English uses have + past participle, but Korean marks it by literally saying 'there is an experience of trying'. For 'never', use eopseoyo (\uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694).","\uc800\ub294 \uc81c\uc8fc\ub3c4\uc5d0 \uac00 \ubcf8 \uc801\uc774 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun jejudoe ga bon jeogi isseoyo.","I have been to Jeju Island.","TOPIK 1","Don't use plain past -asseoyo (\uc558\uc5b4\uc694) for life experience, since it doesn't say that you did it before.",!0,"\ubcf8 \uc801\uc774",A.VB,!0,1)
A.dBz=s(["\ud558\uc744\uac8c\uc694","\ud574\uac8c\uc694","\ud560\uaed8\uc694"],t.s)
A.b4_=new B.P("ko:g-promise-lgeyo",2,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\u3139\uac8c\uc694","I will ... (promise, decision)","Use it to promise or tell someone what you will do. Add -lgeyo (\u3139\uac8c\uc694) to a stem that ends in a vowel (jeonhwahal-geyo (\uc804\ud654\ud560\uac8c\uc694)) and -eulgeyo (\uc744\uac8c\uc694) to a stem that ends in a consonant (meogeul-geyo (\uba39\uc744\uac8c\uc694)), and \u3139 stems just add -geyo (\uac8c\uc694) (mandeul-geyo (\ub9cc\ub4e4\uac8c\uc694)). English uses 'I'll', but Korean only uses it for yourself, and not for questions about 'you' or for other people.","\ub0b4\uc77c \uc804\ud654\ud560\uac8c\uc694.","naeil jeonhwahalgeyo.","I'll call you tomorrow.","TOPIK 1","Don't use it for someone else's actions, since it only means 'I will' (or 'we will'), and always write -geyo (\uac8c\uc694) with \u3131, even though it sounds like -kkeyo (\uaed8\uc694).",!0,"\ud560\uac8c\uc694",A.dBz,!0,1)
A.cxL=s(["\ub9cc\ub098\uc73c\ub824\uace0","\ub9cc\ub0a0\ub824\uace0","\ub9cc\ub098\uc73c\ub824"],t.s)
A.b1S=new B.P("ko:g-intend-ryeogo",2,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\ub824\uace0 \ud574\uc694","plan to / intend to","Use it to say what you are planning to do. Add -ryeogo haeyo (\ub824\uace0 \ud574\uc694) to a stem that ends in a vowel (mannaryeogo haeyo (\ub9cc\ub098\ub824\uace0 \ud574\uc694)) and -euryeogo haeyo (\uc73c\ub824\uace0 \ud574\uc694) to a stem that ends in a consonant (ilg-euryeogo haeyo (\uc77d\uc73c\ub824\uace0 \ud574\uc694)). English says 'I'm planning to', and Korean does too, but it never needs a separate word for 'I', and \u3139 stems just add -ryeogo (\ub824\uace0) and keep the \u3139: mandeulda (\ub9cc\ub4e4\ub2e4) becomes mandeullyeogo (\ub9cc\ub4e4\ub824\uace0), not mandeuryeogo (\ub9cc\ub4dc\ub824\uace0).","\uc8fc\ub9d0\uc5d0 \uce5c\uad6c\ub97c \ub9cc\ub098\ub824\uace0 \ud574\uc694.","jumare chingureul mannaryeogo haeyo.","I'm planning to meet a friend on the weekend.","TOPIK 1","Don't add -eu (\uc73c) after a vowel: mannaeuryeogo is wrong, mannaryeogo is right (\ub9cc\ub098\ub824\uace0).",!0,"\ub9cc\ub098\ub824\uace0",A.cxL,!0,1)
A.dzm=s(["\uc624\uc138\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694","\uc624\uc2dc\uc558\uc5b4\uc694","\uc624\uc0dc\uc5b4\uc694"],t.s)
A.b_h=new B.P("ko:g-honorific-past",13,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\uc168\uc5b4\uc694","honorific past tense","Use it to talk about something a respected person did. Add -syeosseoyo (\uc168\uc5b4\uc694) to a stem that ends in a vowel (o-syeosseoyo (\uc624\uc168\uc5b4\uc694)) and -eusyeosseoyo (\uc73c\uc168\uc5b4\uc694) to a stem that ends in a consonant (ilg-eusyeosseoyo (\uc77d\uc73c\uc168\uc5b4\uc694)). It combines -(eu)si (\uc73c\uc2dc) with the past. English has no honorific verbs at all, so you must choose it by who the subject is, and never use it for yourself.","\uc120\uc0dd\ub2d8\uc774 \uc5b4\uc81c \uc624\uc168\uc5b4\uc694.","seonsaengnimi eoje osyeosseoyo.","The teacher came yesterday.","TOPIK 1","Don't use it for yourself, only for the person you respect (teachers, elders, customers), and a few verbs have special forms like deusida (\ub4dc\uc2dc\ub2e4, 'to eat') and jumusida (\uc8fc\ubb34\uc2dc\ub2e4, 'to sleep').",!0,"\uc624\uc168\uc5b4\uc694",A.dzm,!0,1)
A.dey=s(["\uacf5\ubd80\ud558\uc2b5\ub2c8\ub2e4","\uacf5\ubd80\ud569\uc2b5\ub2c8\ub2e4","\uacf5\ubd80\ud568\ub2c8\ub2e4"],t.s)
A.b2f=new B.P("ko:g-formal-polite",13,"\ucd08\uae09","-\u3142\ub2c8\ub2e4/\uc2b5\ub2c8\ub2e4","formal polite style (statement)","Use it in formal places like news, presentations, work, and the military. Add -mnida (\u3142\ub2c8\ub2e4) to a stem that ends in a vowel (gam-nida (\uac11\ub2c8\ub2e4)) and -seumnida (\uc2b5\ub2c8\ub2e4) to a stem that ends in a consonant (meog-seumnida (\uba39\uc2b5\ub2c8\ub2e4)), and \u3139 stems drop the \u3139: salda (\uc0b4\ub2e4) becomes samnida (\uc0bd\ub2c8\ub2e4). English only changes word choice for formality, but Korean has a whole ending for it, and questions use -mnikka (\u3142\ub2c8\uae4c) or -seumnikka (\uc2b5\ub2c8\uae4c).","\uc800\ub294 \ud55c\uad6d\uc5b4\ub97c \uacf5\ubd80\ud569\ub2c8\ub2e4.","jeoneun hangugeoreul gongbuhamnida.","I study Korean.","TOPIK 1","Use -mnida/-seumnida (\u3142\ub2c8\ub2e4/\uc2b5\ub2c8\ub2e4) in formal settings and -ayo/-eoyo (\uc544\uc694/\uc5b4\uc694) in everyday polite talk.",!0,"\uacf5\ubd80\ud569\ub2c8\ub2e4",A.dey,!0,1)
A.dDK=s(["\uba39\uc544","\uba39\uc73c\uc5b4","\uba39\uc5ec"],t.s)
A.b3F=new B.P("ko:g-banmal",13,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc544/\uc5b4 (\ubc18\ub9d0)","casual speech (without -yo)","Use it with close friends, family, and people younger than you. Take the polite -ayo/-eoyo (\uc544\uc694/\uc5b4\uc694) form and drop -yo (\uc694): meogeo (\uba39\uc5b4), ga (\uac00), joa (\uc88b\uc544). It makes the same statement or question, and only your tone changes. English has no such difference, so using it with a boss, a stranger, or an elder can sound rude.","\ub098 \uc9c0\uae08 \ubc25 \uba39\uc5b4.","na jigeum bap meogeo.","I'm eating now.","TOPIK 1","Don't use it with someone older or someone you just met unless they say it's fine, since it can sound rude.",!0,"\uba39\uc5b4",A.dDK,!0,1)
A.Ab=s(["\uace0","\uc11c","\ub97c"],t.s)
A.b2P=new B.P("ko:g-mot-ji",10,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc9c0 \ubabb\ud574\uc694","can't / am not able to","Use it to say that you are unable to do something. Take the verb stem and add -ji mothaeyo (\uc9c0 \ubabb\ud574\uc694) (suyeonghaji motaeyo (\uc218\uc601\ud558\uc9c0 \ubabb\ud574\uc694)), with no change for sounds. It's the long form of mot (\ubabb) + verb, and it often sounds a bit more formal. English says can't, but Korean also uses it when something stops you, like time or health, and not only when you lack skill.","\uc800\ub294 \uc218\uc601\ud558\uc9c0 \ubabb\ud574\uc694.","jeoneun suyeonghaji motaeyo.","I can't swim.","TOPIK 1","Don't mix it up with -ji anayo (\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694), which means you don't do it by choice.",!0,"\uc9c0",A.Ab,!0,1)
A.ML=s(["\ub9d0\uc138\uc694","\ub9c8\uc138\uc624","\ub9d9\uc138\uc694"],t.s)
A.b10=new B.P("ko:g-prohibit",9,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc9c0 \ub9c8\uc138\uc694","please don't ...","Use it to politely tell someone not to do something. Take the verb stem and add -ji maseyo (\uc9c0 \ub9c8\uc138\uc694) (jjikji maseyo (\ucc0d\uc9c0 \ub9c8\uc138\uc694)), with no change for sounds. It's the opposite of -(eu)seyo (\uc73c\uc138\uc694) and it's polite, though it can sound strong in a sign or a warning. English says 'don't', and Korean puts ma (\ub9c8) in the middle of the verb form.","\uc5ec\uae30\uc11c \uc0ac\uc9c4\uc744 \ucc0d\uc9c0 \ub9c8\uc138\uc694.","yeogiseo sajineul jjikji maseyo.","Please don't take photos here.","TOPIK 1","Don't write malseyo (\ub9d0\uc138\uc694), since the correct spelling is maseyo (\ub9c8\uc138\uc694).",!0,"\ub9c8\uc138\uc694",A.ML,!0,1)
A.bSx=s(["\uba39\uc544 \ubcf4\uc138\uc694","\uba39\uc5b4 \ubd10\uc138\uc694","\uba39\uc73c \ubcf4\uc138\uc694"],t.s)
A.b1F=new B.P("ko:g-try-boda",9,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc544/\uc5b4 \ubcf4\uc138\uc694","please try ...ing","Use it to invite or suggest that someone try something. Take the verb stem, add -a/-eo (\uc544/\uc5b4) with the same vowel rule as -ayo/-eoyo (\uc544\uc694/\uc5b4\uc694), then add boseyo (\ubcf4\uc138\uc694) (meogeo boseyo (\uba39\uc5b4 \ubcf4\uc138\uc694)). English says 'try eating' or 'give it a try', and Korean uses the verb bo (\ubcf4, 'to see') as a helper to mean 'try and see'.","\uc774 \uae40\uce58\ub97c \uba39\uc5b4 \ubcf4\uc138\uc694.","i gimchireul meogeo boseyo.","Please try this kimchi.","TOPIK 1","Don't write bwaseyo (\ubd10\uc138\uc694), since the correct form is boseyo (\ubcf4\uc138\uc694).",!0,"\uba39\uc5b4 \ubcf4\uc138\uc694",A.bSx,!0,1)
A.dPl=s(["\uc608\uc058\uc544\uc9c0\ub9cc","\uc608\uc05c\uc9c0\ub9cc","\uc608\uc058\uc73c\uc9c0\ub9cc"],t.s)
A.b3A=new B.P("ko:g-but-jiman",14,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc9c0\ub9cc","but / although","Use it to join two ideas that contrast: A is true, but B is also true. Attach -jiman (\uc9c0\ub9cc) straight to the verb or adjective stem, whatever the last sound is (yeppeu-jiman (\uc608\uc058\uc9c0\ub9cc), eoryeop-jiman (\uc5b4\ub835\uc9c0\ub9cc)). For the past, put -ass-/-eoss- (\uc558/\uc5c8) before it (joh-assjiman (\uc88b\uc558\uc9c0\ub9cc)). English puts 'but' between two clauses, while Korean keeps one sentence and just swaps the ending, and the subject is often dropped.","\uc774 \uc637\uc740 \uc608\uc058\uc9c0\ub9cc \ube44\uc2f8\uc694.","i oseun yeppeujiman bissayo.","This outfit is pretty, but it's expensive.","TOPIK 1","It joins two clauses inside one sentence, so don't put it at the start of a new sentence; use hajiman (\ud558\uc9c0\ub9cc) for that.",!0,"\uc608\uc058\uc9c0\ub9cc",A.dPl,!0,1)
A.ec4=s(["\uc624\uc73c\ub2c8\uae4c","\uc640\ub2c8\uae4c","\uc624\uc5b4\ub2c8\uae4c"],t.s)
A.aZP=new B.P("ko:g-because-nikka",14,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\ub2c8\uae4c","because / since (reason)","Use it to give a reason, especially when the next part is a suggestion or a command (let's..., please...). Add -nikka (\ub2c8\uae4c) after a stem that ends in a vowel (o-nikka (\uc624\ub2c8\uae4c)) and -eunikka (\uc73c\ub2c8\uae4c) after a stem that ends in a consonant (meog-eunikka (\uba39\uc73c\ub2c8\uae4c)); for the past, use -asseunikka/-eosseunikka (\uc558\uc73c\ub2c8\uae4c/\uc5c8\uc73c\ub2c8\uae4c). English 'because' works for everything, but -aseo/-eoseo (\uc544\uc11c/\uc5b4\uc11c) can't be followed by 'let's' or 'please', so use -nikka in those cases.","\ube44\uac00 \uc624\ub2c8\uae4c \uc6b0\uc0b0\uc744 \uac00\uc838\uac00\uc138\uc694.","biga onikka usaneul gajyeogaseyo.","Since it's raining, please take an umbrella.","TOPIK 1","Use -nikka (\ub2c8\uae4c) before a suggestion or command and -aseo (\uc544\uc11c) for plain cause and result.",!0,"\uc624\ub2c8\uae4c",A.ec4,!0,1)
A.d1l=s(["\uc788\uba74","\uc788\uc73c\uc73c\uba74","\uc788\uc5b4\uba74"],t.s)
A.b3i=new B.P("ko:g-if-myeon",14,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\uba74","if / when (condition)","Use it for conditions: if A happens, then B. Add -myeon (\uba74) after a vowel (o-myeon (\uc624\uba74)) and -eumyeon (\uc73c\uba74) after a consonant (meog-eumyeon (\uba39\uc73c\uba74)); the past is -asseumyeon/-eosseumyeon (\uc558\uc73c\uba74/\uc5c8\uc73c\uba74). English puts 'if' at the start, but Korean puts the ending at the end of the condition clause, and the main clause comes last.","\uc2dc\uac04\uc774 \uc788\uc73c\uba74 \uc800\ud55c\ud14c \uc804\ud654\ud558\uc138\uc694.","sigani isseumyeon jeohante jeonhwahaseyo.","If you have time, please call me.","TOPIK 1","Korean doesn't need a separate word for 'if' (manyak (\ub9cc\uc57d) is optional), because the ending carries the meaning.",!0,"\uc788\uc73c\uba74",A.d1l,!0,1)
A.eqn=s(["\uac00\uc744 \ub54c","\uac00 \ub54c","\uac08 \uc5d0"],t.s)
A.aYR=new B.P("ko:g-when-ttae",14,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\u3139 \ub54c","when / at the time of","Use it to say 'when' something happens. Add -l ttae (\u3139 \ub54c) to a vowel stem (ga-l ttae (\uac08 \ub54c)) and -eul ttae (\uc744 \ub54c) to a consonant stem (meog-eul ttae (\uba39\uc744 \ub54c)); after a noun, just add ttae (\ub54c) (banghak ttae (\ubc29\ud559 \ub54c), 'during vacation'), and adjectives work too (eoril ttae (\uc5b4\ub9b4 \ub54c), 'when I was little'). English 'when' comes first, but Korean puts ttae (\ub54c) after the clause, and the main verb's tense shows when the whole sentence happens.","\ud55c\uad6d\uc5d0 \uac08 \ub54c \uc120\ubb3c\uc744 \uc0ac\uc694.","hanguge gal ttae seonmureul sayo.","When I go to Korea, I buy gifts.","TOPIK 1","For a finished event, add the past ending first: gass-eul ttae (\uac14\uc744 \ub54c) means 'when I went', while gal ttae (\uac08 \ub54c) means 'when I go' or 'when I'm about to go'.",!0,"\uac08 \ub54c",A.eqn,!0,1)
A.elU=s(["\ub4e3\uba74\uc11c","\ub4e4\uc5b4\uba74\uc11c","\ub4e3\uc73c\uba74\uc11c"],t.s)
A.b20=new B.P("ko:g-while-myeonseo",14,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\uba74\uc11c","while (doing two things)","Use it when one person does two things at the same time. Add -myeonseo (\uba74\uc11c) after a vowel (masi-myeonseo (\ub9c8\uc2dc\uba74\uc11c)) and -eumyeonseo (\uc73c\uba74\uc11c) after a consonant (meog-eumyeonseo (\uba39\uc73c\uba74\uc11c)). The subject has to be the same for both actions, and English 'while' also does this, but Korean puts the ending on the first verb and keeps the main verb last.","\uc74c\uc545\uc744 \ub4e4\uc73c\uba74\uc11c \uacf5\ubd80\ud574\uc694.","eumageul deureumyeonseo gongbuhaeyo.","I study while listening to music.","TOPIK 1","Both actions need the same subject, so don't use it to say 'while my sister cooks, I study', and a few stems change, like deutda (\ub4e3\ub2e4) becoming deureumyeonseo (\ub4e4\uc73c\uba74\uc11c).",!0,"\ub4e4\uc73c\uba74\uc11c",A.elU,!0,1)
A.eOm=s(["\uc794 \uc804\uc5d0","\uc790\ub294 \uc804\uc5d0","\uc790\uc744 \uc804\uc5d0"],t.s)
A.b1W=new B.P("ko:g-before-gi-jeone",14,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uae30 \uc804\uc5d0","before (doing)","Use it to say 'before doing something'. Add -gi jeone (\uae30 \uc804\uc5d0) straight to the verb stem, whatever the last sound is (ja-gi jeone (\uc790\uae30 \uc804\uc5d0), meok-gi jeone (\uba39\uae30 \uc804\uc5d0)); with a noun, use the noun plus jeone (\uc804\uc5d0) (sueop jeone (\uc218\uc5c5 \uc804\uc5d0)). English uses a verb with -ing after 'before', and Korean does the same with -gi (\uae30), but it never changes for the past, even when the main verb is in the past.","\uc790\uae30 \uc804\uc5d0 \uc774\ub97c \ub2e6\uc544\uc694.","jagi jeone ireul dakkayo.","I brush my teeth before I go to bed.","TOPIK 1","The part before jeone (\uc804\uc5d0) always stays in the plain -gi (\uae30) form, so don't add a past ending there.",!0,"\uc790\uae30 \uc804\uc5d0",A.eOm,!0,1)
A.e4x=s(["\ub05d\ub098\uae30 \ud6c4\uc5d0","\ub05d\ub098\ub294 \ud6c4\uc5d0","\ub05d\ub0a0 \ud6c4\uc5d0"],t.s)
A.b0g=new B.P("ko:g-after-n-hue",14,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\u3134 \ud6c4\uc5d0","after (doing)","Use it to say 'after doing something'. Add -n hue (\u3134 \ud6c4\uc5d0) to a vowel stem (gongbuha-n hue (\uacf5\ubd80\ud55c \ud6c4\uc5d0), from gongbuhada (\uacf5\ubd80\ud558\ub2e4)) and -eun hue (\uc740 \ud6c4\uc5d0) to a consonant stem (meog-eun hue (\uba39\uc740 \ud6c4\uc5d0)). It always takes the past form, even when the whole sentence is about the future, and 'after' in English works the same way with -ing, but Korean needs this -n/-eun (\u3134/\uc740) form, not -gi (\uae30).","\uc218\uc5c5\uc774 \ub05d\ub09c \ud6c4\uc5d0 \uc810\uc2ec\uc744 \uba39\uc5b4\uc694.","sueobi kkeunnan hue jeomsimeul meogeoyo.","I eat lunch after class ends.","TOPIK 1","Don't use -gi hue (\uae30 \ud6c4\uc5d0): after uses the past form -eun hue (\uc740 \ud6c4\uc5d0), but before uses -gi jeone (\uae30 \uc804\uc5d0).",!0,"\ub05d\ub09c \ud6c4\uc5d0",A.e4x,!0,1)
A.dqp=s(["\ubcf4\uc5b4\uac70\ub098","\ubcf4\uc73c\uac70\ub098","\ubcf4\uac70\ub108"],t.s)
A.b_l=new B.P("ko:g-or-geona",14,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uac70\ub098","or (choosing between actions)","Use it to say 'do A or B'. Attach -geona (\uac70\ub098) straight to the verb or adjective stem, whatever the last sound is (bo-geona (\ubcf4\uac70\ub098), ilg-geona (\uc77d\uac70\ub098)); there is no vowel harmony to think about. English puts 'or' between two verbs, and Korean puts -geona (\uac70\ub098) on the first verb and keeps the tense on the last one only.","\uc8fc\ub9d0\uc5d0\ub294 \uc601\ud654\ub97c \ubcf4\uac70\ub098 \uc26c\uc5b4\uc694.","jumareneun yeonghwareul bogeona swieoyo.","On weekends I watch a movie or rest.","TOPIK 1","For two nouns, use -(i)na (\uc774\ub098) instead (chaek-ina yeonghwa (\ucc45\uc774\ub098 \uc601\ud654)), because -geona (\uac70\ub098) is only for verbs and adjectives.",!0,"\ubcf4\uac70\ub098",A.dqp,!0,1)
A.dCK=s(["\uc77c\uc5b4\ub098\uc544\uc57c","\uc77c\uc5b4\ub098\uc5b4\uc57c","\uc77c\uc5b4\ub098\uc73c\uc57c"],t.s)
A.b12=new B.P("ko:g-must",15,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc544/\uc5b4\uc57c \ud574\uc694","have to / must","Use it to say what you must do. Take the stem and add -aya (\uc544\uc57c) if its last vowel is a or o, and -eoya (\uc5b4\uc57c) for all other vowels, then add haeyo (\ud574\uc694) (meog-eoya haeyo (\uba39\uc5b4\uc57c \ud574\uc694), hae-ya haeyo (\ud574\uc57c \ud574\uc694)). You can also end with dwaeyo (\ub3fc\uc694) instead of haeyo (\ud574\uc694), with the same meaning. English has must or have to as separate words, but Korean puts the obligation in the verb ending, so the main verb always comes first.","\ub0b4\uc77c \uc77c\ucc0d \uc77c\uc5b4\ub098\uc57c \ud574\uc694.","naeil iljjik ireonaya haeyo.","I have to get up early tomorrow.","TOPIK 1","The vowel rule only looks at the last vowel of the stem, and hada (\ud558\ub2e4) verbs become haeya (\ud574\uc57c).",!0,"\uc77c\uc5b4\ub098\uc57c",A.dCK,!0,1)
A.d0k=s(["\ucc0d\uc544\ub3c4","\ucc0d\ub3c4","\ucc0d\uc73c\ub3c4"],t.s)
A.b21=new B.P("ko:g-may",15,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc544/\uc5b4\ub3c4 \ub3fc\uc694","may / it's okay to","Use it to ask for or give permission. Take the stem and add -ado (\uc544\ub3c4) if its last vowel is a or o, and -eodo (\uc5b4\ub3c4) for all other vowels, then add dwaeyo (\ub3fc\uc694) (jjig-eodo dwaeyo (\ucc0d\uc5b4\ub3c4 \ub3fc\uc694)). With a question tone, it means 'is it okay if I...?'. English uses 'can' or 'may' as a separate word, but Korean puts the permission in the verb ending, and ga-do (\uac00\ub3c4) is short for ga-ado (\uac00\uc544\ub3c4), since the same vowel merges.","\uc5ec\uae30\uc5d0\uc11c \uc0ac\uc9c4\uc744 \ucc0d\uc5b4\ub3c4 \ub3fc\uc694?","yeogieseo sajineul jjigeodo dwaeyo?","Can I take photos here?","TOPIK 1","Don't say -ado (\uc544\ub3c4) after every vowel: use -eodo (\uc5b4\ub3c4) unless the last vowel is a or o.",!0,"\ucc0d\uc5b4\ub3c4",A.d0k,!0,1)
A.ey9=s(["\ud53c\uc6b0\uc73c\uba74","\ud53c\uc6cc\uba74","\ud53c\uc6b0\uc5b4\uba74"],t.s)
A.b2u=new B.P("ko:g-must-not",15,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\uba74 \uc548 \ub3fc\uc694","must not / not allowed to","Use it to say that something isn't allowed. Add -myeon (\uba74) after a vowel stem (pi-u-myeon (\ud53c\uc6b0\uba74)) and -eumyeon (\uc73c\uba74) after a consonant stem (meog-eumyeon (\uba39\uc73c\uba74)), then add an dwaeyo (\uc548 \ub3fc\uc694). English says 'you can't' or 'you must not' as one block, but Korean builds it from 'if you do it' plus 'that doesn't work', so the verb comes first.","\uc5ec\uae30\uc5d0\uc11c \ub2f4\ubc30\ub97c \ud53c\uc6b0\uba74 \uc548 \ub3fc\uc694.","yeogieseo dambaereul piumyeon an dwaeyo.","You can't smoke here.","TOPIK 1","It's the opposite of -ado dwaeyo (\uc544\ub3c4 \ub3fc\uc694): the first one means 'it's okay', and this one means 'it's not allowed'.",!0,"\ud53c\uc6b0\uba74",A.ey9,!0,1)
A.cCe=s(["\uac00\ub974\uce5c\ub294","\uac00\ub974\uce58\uc740","\uac00\ub974\uce58\uc73c\ub294"],t.s)
A.b_L=new B.P("ko:g-modifier-present",16,"\ucd08\uae09","-\ub294 + \uba85\uc0ac","-ing / who (verb) (present, before a noun)","Use it to describe a noun with a verb: 'the person who teaches', 'the food I'm eating'. Add -neun (\ub294) to the verb stem, whatever the last sound is (meok-neun (\uba39\ub294), ga-neun (\uac00\ub294)); verbs that end in l (\u3139) drop it first (sa-neun (\uc0ac\ub294), 'who lives'). English puts the description after the noun ('a caf\xe9 that I go to'), but Korean puts it before the noun, and the verb always sits right in front of the noun.","\uc800\ub294 \ud55c\uad6d\uc5b4\ub97c \uac00\ub974\uce58\ub294 \uc120\uc0dd\ub2d8\uc774\uc5d0\uc694.","jeoneun hangugeoreul gareuchineun seonsaengnimieyo.","I'm a teacher who teaches Korean.","TOPIK 1","The whole description goes before the noun, so Korean word order is the reverse of 'the caf\xe9 that I often visit'.",!0,"\uac00\ub974\uce58\ub294",A.cCe,!0,1)
A.cK0=s(["\ubcf4\uc740","\ubcf8\uc740","\ubcf4\uc5b8"],t.s)
A.aZ_=new B.P("ko:g-modifier-past",16,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\u3134 + \uba85\uc0ac / \ud615\uc6a9\uc0ac + \uba85\uc0ac","past verb + noun / adjective + noun","Use it for a finished action before a noun (the movie I saw) and also for adjectives before a noun (a small bag). Add -n (\u3134) to a vowel stem (bo-n (\ubcf8)) and -eun (\uc740) to a consonant stem (jag-eun (\uc791\uc740)); with adjectives, this form is the present tense, and with verbs it's the past. English uses separate words for 'which' and the tense, but Korean puts everything in one ending before the noun.","\uc5b4\uc81c \ubcf8 \uc601\ud654\uac00 \uc7ac\ubbf8\uc788\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694.","eoje bon yeonghwaga jaemiisseosseoyo.","The movie I saw yesterday was fun.","TOPIK 1","Same shape, different job: a verb with -n/-eun (\u3134/\uc740) is past, but an adjective with it is the present (jag-eun gabang (\uc791\uc740 \uac00\ubc29), 'small bag').",!0,"\ubcf8",A.cK0,!0,1)
A.bXs=s(["\uc624\uc744 \uac83","\uc624 \uac83","\uc62c \uac83\uc744"],t.s)
A.b36=new B.P("ko:g-seems",12,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\u3139 \uac83 \uac19\uc544\uc694","I think / it seems (a guess)","Use it to give a guess or a soft opinion. Add -l geot gatayo (\u3139 \uac83 \uac19\uc544\uc694) to a vowel stem (o-l geot gatayo (\uc62c \uac83 \uac19\uc544\uc694)) and -eul geot gatayo (\uc744 \uac83 \uac19\uc544\uc694) to a consonant stem (maeu-l geot gatayo (\ub9e4\uc6b8 \uac83 \uac19\uc544\uc694), from maepda (\ub9f5\ub2e4)); after a noun, use -il geot gatayo (\uc77c \uac83 \uac19\uc544\uc694). English says 'I think' or 'it looks like', and Korean often uses this ending to sound less direct, which is polite.","\ub0b4\uc77c \ube44\uac00 \uc62c \uac83 \uac19\uc544\uc694.","naeil biga ol geot gatayo.","I think it'll rain tomorrow.","TOPIK 1","Use -n/-neun geot gatayo (\u3134/\ub294 \uac83 \uac19\uc544\uc694) when you judge from what you see now or from the past, and -l geot gatayo (\u3139 \uac83 \uac19\uc544\uc694) for guesses about the future or things you aren't sure of.",!0,"\uc62c \uac83",A.bXs,!0,1)
A.bT8=s(["\uc798\ud558\ub0b4\uc694","\uc798\ud558\ub294\ub124\uc694","\uc798\ud558\uc73c\ub124\uc694"],t.s)
A.b2O=new B.P("ko:g-ne-yo",2,"\ucd08\uae09","-\ub124\uc694","oh, I see! (noticing something now)","Use it when you notice or discover something right now and react to it: 'oh, it's snowing!' or 'wow, you're good at it!'. Attach -neyo (\ub124\uc694) straight to the stem, whatever the last sound is (o-neyo (\uc624\ub124\uc694), joh-neyo (\uc88b\ub124\uc694)); for the past, use -ass-neyo/-eoss-neyo (\uc558\ub124\uc694/\uc5c8\ub124\uc694). English uses 'wow' or 'oh' and a tone of voice, but Korean puts that feeling in the ending, and it's often used to give a gentle compliment.","\uc640, \ud55c\uad6d\uc5b4\ub97c \uc798\ud558\ub124\uc694!","wa, hangugeoreul jalhaneyo!","Wow, you speak Korean well!","TOPIK 1","Use it only for things you've just noticed, so don't use it to talk about your own plans or facts you've known for a long time.",!0,"\uc798\ud558\ub124\uc694",A.bT8,!0,1)
A.ecB=s(["\uc788\uc5b4\uc8e0","\uc788\ub294\uc8e0","\uc788\uc8fc\uc694"],t.s)
A.b_s=new B.P("ko:g-jyo",2,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc8e0?","right? / isn't it? (checking)","Use it to check something you already believe is true: 'you have time tomorrow, right?'. Attach -jyo (\uc8e0) straight to the stem, whatever the last sound is (it-jyo (\uc788\uc8e0), eoryeop-jyo (\uc5b4\ub835\uc8e0)); after a noun, use -ijyo (\uc774\uc8e0) after a consonant (seonsaengnim-ijyo (\uc120\uc0dd\ub2d8\uc774\uc8e0)). With a rising tone it's a question, and with a falling tone it means 'you know...'. English adds 'right?' or 'isn't it?' with different words each time, but Korean uses this one ending every time.","\ub0b4\uc77c \uc2dc\uac04 \uc788\uc8e0?","naeil sigan itjyo?","You have time tomorrow, right?","TOPIK 1","It's a short form of -ji yo (\uc9c0\uc694), and the shape never changes for I, you, or they.",!0,"\uc788\uc8e0",A.ecB,!0,1)
A.f1o=s(["\uadf8\ub9ac\uac70","\uadf8\ub9ad\uace0","\uadf8\ub9ac\uc624"],t.s)
A.b_p=new B.P("ko:g-connectors",7,"\ucd08\uae09","\uadf8\ub9ac\uace0\xb7\uadf8\ub798\uc11c\xb7\ud558\uc9c0\ub9cc\xb7\uadf8\ub7f0\ub370","and / so / but / by the way (sentence connectors)","Use them at the start of a new sentence to link it to the one before. Geurigo (\uadf8\ub9ac\uace0) adds more information ('and'), geuraeseo (\uadf8\ub798\uc11c) gives a result ('so'), hajiman (\ud558\uc9c0\ub9cc) shows a contrast ('but'), and geureonde (\uadf8\ub7f0\ub370) changes the subject or adds a surprise ('by the way', 'but'). They never change shape, and English 'and' or 'but' can sit in the middle of a sentence, while these four usually start a new sentence.","\uc800\ub294 \uc544\uce68\uc5d0 \uc6b4\ub3d9\ud574\uc694. \uadf8\ub9ac\uace0 \ud559\uad50\uc5d0 \uac00\uc694.","jeoneun achime undonghaeyo. geurigo hakgyoe gayo.","I work out in the morning. And then I go to school.","TOPIK 1","Don't mix them up with -go (\uace0) or -jiman (\uc9c0\ub9cc) inside a sentence: the connectors start a new sentence, so a period comes before them.",!0,"\uadf8\ub9ac\uace0",A.f1o,!0,1)
